  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "The vertex of the parabola $y=a{x}^{2}+bx+c$ is at the point $(-1,-1).$ Given that $D$ is the parabola's discriminant and $a<0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $c<0$\n\nII. $D>0$\n\nIII. $D<0$",
  "worked_solution": "We know the location of the vertex and that it is downward-opening. So, let's sketch our parabola based on this information.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14266874/assets/0d6b4ae9f3acd662a7238108c4711d08f0c245bed0188bc9dbc31f274ddaa81c.png)\n\nNow, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $a<0,$ the parabola opens downward. Moreover, since the vertex $(-1,-1)$ is below the $x$-axis, the $y$-intercept is also below the $x$-axis. Therefore, we must have $c<0.$\n\n- Statement II is false, and statement III is true. Since the parabola opens downward and its vertex is below the $x$-axis, it doesn't intersect the $x$-axis. Hence, it does not have any roots. Therefore, we must have $D<0.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true.",
